DOMAIN: Key Theoretical Debates in Historical Materialism
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-03-28
PAPERS_FOUND: 13 total (High: 7, Medium: 4, Low: 2)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, CrossRef
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
The theoretical debates within historical materialism cluster around
five interconnected axes. First, the base/superstructure metaphor:
whether economic relations causally determine legal, political, and
ideological forms (hard determinism), or whether the superstructure
possesses relative autonomy that can react back on the base
(Althusser, Williams, Wood). Second, the "primacy of productive
forces" thesis (G.A. Cohen's 1978 KMTH): that productive forces
explain the character of production relations, and that their
development tendency explains the succession of modes of production.
This thesis provoked a sharp counter from the social relations school
(Brenner, Wood), who argue that class conflict and property relations
cannot be derived from technological imperatives. Third, the
teleology question: does historical materialism presuppose an
immanent directional logic (toward communism) that commits it to
historical inevitability, or can it be reconstructed as a
non-teleological explanatory framework? Cohen's functional
explanation defense and Honderich's critique are the locus classicus
here. Fourth, the agency/structure tension: does historical
materialism subordinate individual and collective agency to structural
determination, or can class struggle be theorized as the engine of
history (Thompson vs Althusser; Callinicos's synthesis)? Fifth, the
unilinear vs multilinear debate: whether all societies pass through
the same sequence of modes of production (the classical "five stages"
doctrine) or whether Marx himself allowed for multiple developmental
paths, as suggested by his late writings on Russia and the Asiatic
mode of production.

The field's central reference point is Cohen (1978), which catalyzed
the "analytical Marxism" program. Brenner's empirical-historical
challenge to Cohen's technological determinism and Wood's political
Marxism represent the main alternative tradition. Recent scholarship
(Chibber 2011) attempts to adjudicate what is viable in Cohen's
framework while incorporating the social relations school's insights.
Carling (1993) provides a comprehensive mapping of the analytical
Marxism/historical materialism debate.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain covers the core interpretive controversies that any
reconstruction of Marx's theory of history must navigate: the nature
of historical causation, the role of agency, and the scope of
historical necessity. The base/superstructure, teleology, and
forces/relations debates directly bear on how competing traditions
(Hegelian, analytical, structuralist, humanist) have read Marx.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
The debate on multilinear history and the Asiatic mode of production
is underrepresented in English-language philosophical literature;
most treatments remain primarily historical-sociological rather than
philosophical. Recent Continental reconstructions (Balibar, Negri)
are less well covered by database searches than the analytical
tradition. No entry is included for E.P. Thompson's Poverty of Theory
(the humanist/anti-structuralist pole) since CrossRef could not
verify its metadata.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Structure around the five debate-axes above; treat Cohen (1978) as
the axis of the forces/teleology cluster, and Wood/Brenner as the
axis of the social relations cluster. Callinicos and Chibber provide
bridging positions for the agency/structure debate.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Primacy of Productive Forces (technological determinism): 4 papers -- Cohen KMTH, Cohen functional, Shaw, Carling
- Social Relations School (political Marxism): 4 papers -- Brenner 1976, Brenner 1977, Brenner 1982, Wood
- Relative Autonomy / Western Marxism: 1 paper -- Williams
- Agency and Structure synthesis: 2 papers -- Callinicos, Chibber
- Critical survey: 2 papers -- Honderich, Roberts
====================================================================
}

@book{cohen1978kmth,
  author    = {Cohen, G. A.},
  title     = {Karl Marx's Theory of History: A Defence},
  year      = {1979},
  publisher = {Princeton University Press},
  doi       = {10.1515/9780691213002},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Cohen defends a systematic reconstruction of historical
  materialism centered on the "primacy of productive forces" thesis: the
  character of production relations is explained by their tendency to
  develop the productive forces, and the succession of modes of production
  is explained by the development tendency of those forces. Cohen defends
  functional explanation as a legitimate scientific practice, arguing that
  functional explanations in historical materialism can be grounded without
  specifying the mechanism -- analogously to functional explanation in
  evolutionary biology.

  RELEVANCE: This is the foundational text of analytical Marxism and the
  locus of the central modern debate on Marx's theory of history. Any
  account of how the 20th century read Marx must engage Cohen's
  reconstruction, both as the most rigorous statement of technological
  determinism and as the provocation for the social relations school's
  response. It directly addresses the teleology and base/superstructure
  questions central to this domain.

  POSITION: Analytical Marxism; primacy of productive forces; functional
  explanation defense.
  },
  keywords  = {historical-materialism, productive-forces, primacy-thesis, functional-explanation, analytical-marxism, High},
  abstract = {First published in 1978, this book rapidly established itself as a classic of modern Marxism. In this expanded edition Cohen offers his own account of the history, and the further promise, of analytical Marxism. He also expresses reservations about traditional historical materialism, in the light of which he reconstructs the theory.},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{brenner1976agrarian,
  author    = {Brenner, Robert},
  title     = {Agrarian Class Structure and Economic Development in Pre-Industrial Europe},
  journal   = {Past and Present},
  year      = {1976},
  volume    = {70},
  number    = {1},
  pages     = {30--75},
  doi       = {10.1093/past/70.1.30},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Brenner argues that variations in agrarian economic
  development across pre-industrial Europe cannot be explained by
  demographic or market forces (contra neo-Malthusian and neo-Smithian
  accounts), but are determined by the specific structure of class
  relations and property rights in each region. This constitutes an
  empirically grounded argument for the primacy of social relations of
  production over productive forces in explaining historical development.

  RELEVANCE: This article inaugurated the Brenner debate -- the major
  historical challenge to Cohen's productive forces primacy thesis. It
  represents the social relations school's alternative to technological
  determinism within historical materialism and directly addresses the
  forces vs. relations question.

  POSITION: Social relations school; class relations primacy; critique of
  productive forces determinism.
  },
  keywords  = {historical-materialism, social-relations, Brenner-debate, class-structure, forces-vs-relations, High},
  abstract = {Journal Article AGRARIAN CLASS STRUCTURE AND ECONOMIC DEVELOPMENT IN PRE-INDUSTRIAL EUROPE Get access Robert Brenner Robert Brenner University of CaliforniaLos Angeles Search for other works by this author on: Oxford Academic Google Scholar Past & Present, Volume 70, Issue 1, February 1976, Pages 30–75, https://doi.org/10.1093/past/70.1.30 Published: 01 February 1976},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

@article{brenner1977origins,
  author    = {Brenner, Robert},
  title     = {The Origins of Capitalist Development: A Critique of Neo-Smithian Marxism},
  journal   = {New Left Review},
  year      = {1977},
  volume    = {1},
  number    = {104},
  pages     = {25--92},
  doi       = {10.64590/9sc},
  note      = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Brenner critiques "neo-Smithian Marxism" -- theories
  (Sweezy, Frank, Wallerstein) that ground capitalist development in
  expanding trade and market relations rather than in the transformation of
  social property relations. He argues that capitalism's origins lie in the
  specific agrarian class structures of early modern England, not in
  commercial expansion per se, and that this transforms the theoretical
  framework for understanding uneven development.

  RELEVANCE: Extends the social relations critique of productive forces
  primacy to the question of capitalism's origins and the transition from
  feudalism, a debate central to multilinear vs. unilinear historical
  materialism. Establishes "political Marxism" as a distinct tradition in
  interpreting Marx's theory of history.

  POSITION: Political Marxism; social property relations; critique of
  world-systems theory and neo-Smithian tendencies.
  },
  keywords  = {social-relations, capitalism-origins, transition-feudalism-capitalism, political-marxism, High},
  abstract = {The appearance of systematic barriers to economic advance in the course of capitalist expansion—the ‘development of underdevelopment’—has posed difficult problems for Marxist theory. There has arisen, in response, a strong tendency sharply to revise Marx’s conceptions regarding economic development. In part, this has been a healthy reaction to . . .},
  abstract_source = {openalex},
}

